<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Third Coalition</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Third Coalition (1805) was fought by Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and its allies against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced an Austrian army to surrender at Ulm and defeated the Austrians and Russians at Austerlitz on 2 December 1805, although Britain won control of the sea at Trafalgar. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> made Austria cede lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and led to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition.jpg" alt="War of the Third Coalition" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruedi33a · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_3rd_coalition.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Peace of Amiens</a> broke down in 1803, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 18 May 1803. Tensions had grown as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> made himself president of the Italian Republic, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and kept French troops in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. After he crowned himself Emperor on 2 December 1804, the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, Parma and Piacenza and his coronation as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> left little doubt about his intentions. On 21 January 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-pitt-the-younger">William Pitt the Younger</a> proposed an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Sweden joined Britain in December 1804, Russia on 11 April 1805 and Austria on 9 August; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> completed the coalition on 11 September. Prussia stayed neutral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> turned the army he had gathered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> for an invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In the Ulm campaign, 210,000 French troops marched east to envelop the Austrian army of General Mack, who had made Ulm the centre of his defence while waiting for the Russians under Mikhail Kutuzov. When Mack heard that Bernadotte's corps had crossed Prussian Ansbach, he chose to stay at Ulm instead of retreating south. Murat forced General Werneck to surrender with 8,000 men at Trochtelfingten on 19 October. Two days later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> defeated the combined Franco-Spanish fleet at Trafalgar.</p><p>At Austerlitz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> the impression that his army was weak and deliberately thinned his right flank, expecting them to weaken their own centre in attacking it. He ordered Davout's corps to march 110 km in 48 hours to support that flank, then sent Soult's IV Corps against the centre, saying, &quot;One sharp blow and the war is over.&quot; In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the French defeated the Austrians at Caldiero between 29 and 31 October. In February 1806 Masséna invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and King Ferdinand IV fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under British naval protection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On 26 December 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, which confirmed Austrian cessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and imposed an indemnity of 40 million francs. After Austerlitz Napoleon created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, whose German states pledged to raise an army of 63,000 men. In 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> abdicated the imperial throne and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> ceased to exist. The French lost 12,000 killed, 22,200 wounded and 5,000 captured in 1805. Peace was not lasting: neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> stopped fighting, and Prussian worries about French influence led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fourth-coalition">War of the Fourth Coalition</a> in 1806.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Third Coalition” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
